POOL.acad.push(
 {id:"ac5", title:"The Placebo Effect",
  text:"In clinical research, a placebo is an inert treatment, such as a sugar pill, given to participants who believe they may be receiving active medication. Remarkably, a substantial proportion of these participants report genuine improvements in symptoms, particularly for conditions involving pain, fatigue or nausea. For decades, researchers regarded this phenomenon merely as a nuisance that had to be controlled for in order to isolate the effect of the actual drug.\n\nMore recent work suggests that placebo responses are not imaginary but reflect measurable physiological changes. Brain imaging studies have shown that expectations of relief can trigger the release of endogenous opioids, the body's own painkillers. Intriguingly, the effect appears to depend on contextual cues: larger pills, branded packaging and even higher prices tend to produce stronger responses. Some studies have reported improvements even when patients were openly told that they were receiving a placebo, a finding that challenges the assumption that deception is essential. Ethicists remain cautious, however, noting that deliberately prescribing placebos could undermine trust between patients and physicians if not handled transparently.",
  qs:[
   {q:"According to paragraph 1, how did researchers traditionally view the placebo effect?", o:["As a promising alternative to medication","As a factor that complicated the evaluation of drugs","As evidence that most symptoms are imaginary","As a technique for reducing pain in surgery"], a:1, ex:"„a nuisance that had to be controlled for“: etwas, das die Bewertung von Medikamenten erschwert."},
   {q:"The word \"endogenous\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to", o:["artificial","produced within the body","dangerous","temporary"], a:1, ex:"Erklärt im Satz selbst: „the body's own painkillers“."},
   {q:"Why does the author mention branded packaging and higher prices?", o:["To show that placebos are expensive to produce","To illustrate that context influences the strength of the effect","To criticize pharmaceutical advertising","To explain why patients prefer real drugs"], a:1, ex:"Beispiele für „contextual cues“, die die Wirkung verstärken."},
   {q:"What does the finding about \"openly\" given placebos challenge?", o:["The idea that placebos only work if patients are misled","The idea that placebos release opioids","The idea that placebos are cheap","The idea that pain cannot be measured"], a:0, ex:"Widerspricht der Annahme, dass Täuschung nötig ist."},
   {q:"Which of the following best describes the organization of the passage?", o:["A traditional view is presented, then revised by newer evidence, and an ethical concern is raised","Two experiments are compared and one is rejected","A historical sequence of drug discoveries is described","A problem is described and a single definitive solution is proposed"], a:0, ex:"Alte Sicht, dann neue Belege, dann ethischer Vorbehalt."}
  ]},
 {id:"ac6", title:"Medieval Trade Fairs",
  text:"Between the twelfth and fourteenth centuries, the fairs of Champagne in northeastern France became the commercial hub of medieval Europe. Six fairs rotated among four towns throughout the year, ensuring that merchants could trade almost continuously. Italian merchants brought spices, silk and dyes from the Mediterranean, while traders from Flanders offered high-quality woolen cloth. The counts of Champagne actively promoted these gatherings by guaranteeing safe conduct to travelers, maintaining roads and establishing special courts that resolved commercial disputes quickly.\n\nPerhaps the fairs' most lasting contribution was financial rather than material. Because merchants were reluctant to carry large quantities of coins over dangerous roads, they increasingly settled accounts through written obligations that could be cleared at the end of each fair. These practices laid the groundwork for later instruments such as bills of exchange. The decline of the fairs in the early fourteenth century is usually attributed to several factors, including the annexation of Champagne by the French crown, which imposed heavier taxes, and the opening of a direct sea route between Italy and Flanders that allowed merchants to bypass the region altogether.",
  qs:[
   {q:"According to paragraph 1, how did the counts of Champagne support the fairs?", o:["By lending money to foreign merchants","By providing security, infrastructure and legal services","By producing woolen cloth","By limiting the number of Italian traders"], a:1, ex:"Geleitschutz, Straßen und spezielle Gerichte."},
   {q:"Why did merchants begin to use written obligations?", o:["Coins were not accepted at the fairs","Carrying large amounts of money was risky","The counts required written contracts","Written records were cheaper to produce"], a:1, ex:"Sie scheuten das Risiko, viel Bargeld über gefährliche Straßen zu tragen."},
   {q:"The phrase \"laid the groundwork for\" is closest in meaning to", o:["replaced","prepared the basis for","prevented the use of","increased the cost of"], a:1, ex:"laid the groundwork = die Grundlage schaffen."},
   {q:"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason for the decline of the fairs?", o:["Higher taxes after the region became part of France","A new maritime route between Italy and Flanders","A decline in the quality of Flemish cloth","Merchants being able to avoid the region"], a:2, ex:"Die Qualität des Tuchs wird nicht als Grund genannt (NOT-Frage)."},
   {q:"What can be inferred about the sea route mentioned in paragraph 2?", o:["It was more dangerous than the land routes","It reduced the need for merchants to meet in Champagne","It was controlled by the counts of Champagne","It was used mainly for transporting coins"], a:1, ex:"Händler konnten die Region umgehen, Champagne wurde also weniger gebraucht."}
  ]}
);
