POOL.ltalk.push(
 {id:"tk7", v:"airy", setting:"Linguistics lecture", title:"Language Death",
  s:"Linguists estimate that roughly seven thousand languages are spoken today, but nearly half of them are considered endangered. A language is usually described as endangered when children are no longer learning it as their first language. Now, it's tempting to think that languages disappear because they're somehow less useful, but that's not really accurate. The main driver is social and economic pressure. When a dominant language is associated with education, jobs and prestige, parents often choose to raise their children in that language, sometimes deliberately, hoping to give them better opportunities. Within two or three generations, the original language may have only elderly speakers left. Why does this matter beyond the speakers themselves? Each language encodes specific knowledge, for instance detailed vocabulary for local plants, weather patterns or navigation, that often isn't documented anywhere else. That said, revitalization is possible. Welsh is the example people usually cite. Through bilingual schooling and media, the number of young speakers has actually grown. But I'd caution against assuming the Welsh model transfers easily, because it depended on strong political support and resources that many small communities simply don't have.",
  qs:[
   {q:"What is the main topic of the lecture?", o:["The history of the Welsh language","Why languages become endangered and whether they can be revived","How children acquire their first language","The number of languages spoken in Europe"], a:1, ex:"Ursachen des Sprachsterbens und Möglichkeiten der Wiederbelebung."},
   {q:"According to the professor, what is the main cause of language endangerment?", o:["Languages becoming grammatically too complex","Social and economic pressure toward a dominant language","A lack of written dictionaries","Government bans on minority languages"], a:1, ex:"„The main driver is social and economic pressure.“"},
   {q:"Why does the professor mention vocabulary for plants and weather?", o:["To show that endangered languages are simpler","To illustrate the unique knowledge languages can contain","To explain how linguists count languages","To argue that dominant languages lack such words"], a:1, ex:"Beispiel für Wissen, das nur in dieser Sprache dokumentiert ist."},
   {q:"What is the professor's attitude toward the Welsh example?", o:["She thinks it proves revitalization always works","She sees it as encouraging but not easily repeated elsewhere","She believes the statistics are inaccurate","She thinks Welsh is still declining"], a:1, ex:"„I'd caution against assuming the Welsh model transfers easily“."}
  ]},
 {id:"tk8", v:"glassy", setting:"Engineering lecture", title:"Why Bridges Sway",
  s:"In the year 2000, a new pedestrian bridge opened in London and had to be closed after only two days because it swayed noticeably from side to side. The engineers had checked the structure for wind and for the weight of the crowd, so what went wrong? It turned out the problem was the pedestrians themselves. When a bridge moves sideways even slightly, people instinctively adjust their walking to keep their balance. They widen their stance and, without realizing it, start stepping in time with the movement. And here's the key point. That synchronized stepping pushes the bridge further in the same direction, which makes the movement larger, which in turn causes more people to fall into step. Engineers call this a positive feedback loop. Interestingly, the effect only appears once the crowd reaches a critical size. Below that threshold, people's random steps cancel each other out. The solution wasn't to rebuild the bridge but to install dampers, devices that absorb the energy of the motion, a bit like shock absorbers in a car. Since then, design codes in many countries require engineers to check footbridges for this kind of crowd behavior.",
  qs:[
   {q:"What is the lecture mainly about?", o:["How wind affects bridge design","How pedestrians unintentionally caused a bridge to sway","The history of bridges in London","Why car shock absorbers were invented"], a:1, ex:"Fußgänger haben das Schwanken unbeabsichtigt verstärkt."},
   {q:"According to the professor, why did the problem appear only with large crowds?", o:["Small groups walked more slowly","Below a certain number, random steps cancel each other out","The bridge was only weak in the middle","Large crowds were heavier than expected"], a:1, ex:"Unterhalb der kritischen Größe heben sich die Schritte auf."},
   {q:"Why does the professor compare dampers to shock absorbers?", o:["To explain how dampers work using a familiar example","To suggest that cars caused the problem","To show that dampers are cheap","To argue that the bridge should be rebuilt"], a:0, ex:"Vertrauter Vergleich zur Erklärung."},
   {q:"What can be inferred about engineering practice after this event?", o:["Footbridges are no longer built in cities","Engineers now consider how crowds move on footbridges","Dampers are banned in new bridges","Wind is no longer tested in design"], a:1, ex:"Normen verlangen jetzt die Prüfung auf solches Verhalten."}
  ]}
);
